window.DUX_SEARCH = [{"path": "00-evidence-and-judgment.html", "title": "Evidence and judgment", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "evidence", "text": "# evidence and judgment\n\ndux asks how people can learn about ai, learn with it, and retain the freedom and capacity to learn without it. an ai-generated answer may be useful or wrong; a fluent answer alone tells us little about whether its reader has learned. the purpose of education can include curiosity, understanding, relationships, participation and independence, not merely a finished answer.\n\n## what the evidence shows\n\nin a turkish high-school mathematics experiment, a gpt-4 interface improved assisted practice scores yet the same group scored lower on a subsequent unassisted exam than the control group. a teacher-informed tutor largely mitigated that deficit, without a detectable positive exam effect. the study concerns one school, four short sessions, and specific tools; it does not show all ai use impairs learning. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n\na harvard undergraduate physics crossover experiment found better immediate post-test scores after a specifically designed ai tutor than after its active-learning class. its sample, course, lessons, setting and design matter; the results cannot establish that ai generally outperforms teachers or that advantages persist. [s08](07-source-records.md#s08)\n\na nigerian pilot found higher assessment scores for an after-school program combining ai access, teacher support and structured content against ordinary schooling. differential assessment completion and the absence of an equal-time human tutoring comparison limit attribution to ai alone. [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n\n## how to read the differences\n\nthese studies ask different questions. the turkish study's subsequent exam was immediately after practice; the physics study compared two modes and topics; the nigerian pilot compared an entire supplemental program with regular schooling. none establishes a universal ranking. a helpful research question is: which learners, doing what work, with what support, compared to what alternative, and measured when?\n\nthe unesco and unicef materials in this library are institutional guidance. their recommendations are useful for designing learning and safeguards, but issuing a framework is not a test of its effectiveness. we label dux suggestions as proposals, not validated interventions. [s02](07-source-records.md#s02) [s03](07-source-records.md#s03) [s05](07-source-records.md#s05)\n\n## where to start\n\nexplore [ai literacy](01-ai-literacy.md), [teaching](02-teaching-and-implementation.md), [independence](03-learning-and-independence.md), or [children](04-children-and-safeguards.md). the [source records](07-source-records.md) preserve access limitations and give direct links. the [method and questions](06-method-and-questions.md) say what remains uncertain.\n", "number": "00"}, {"path": "01-ai-literacy.html", "title": "AI literacy", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "literacy", "text": "# ai literacy\n\nai literacy includes the ability to ask what a system is for, how its output can be checked, whose interests it serves, and who remains accountable. it is broader than prompt writing. unesco’s student framework describes 12 competencies across human-centred mindset, ethics, techniques and applications, and system design, at understand, apply and create levels. it is a framework for curriculum design, not a measured effect of a lesson. [s02](07-source-records.md#s02)\n\n## understanding and questioning\n\nan age-appropriate lesson might compare a generated answer with a reliable source, inspect a plausible error, or discuss how training data and design choices shape outputs. learners can ask who created the system, what information is missing, whether an answer can be checked, and when the task should remain human-led. these are dux teaching proposals; their effectiveness needs evaluation.\n\n## appropriate reliance\n\na small randomized estonian study asked 36 students aged 18–19 to judge fixed screenshots of answers to eight puzzles. a short educational text did not significantly reduce acceptance of incorrect recommendations, and the intervention group rejected more correct recommendations. the experiment did not involve live prompting; its sample and artificial task limit generalization. a non-significant result is not proof of no effect. [s07](07-source-records.md#s07)\n\nthis suggests testing what learners actually do, not only asking whether they remember a warning. compare independently checked work, explanations of reasoning and the ability to identify uncertainty. a learner should be able to accept a justified answer as well as reject an erroneous one. this is a dux inference, not a proven curriculum.\n\n## beyond one age group\n\nschool frameworks need adaptation for adults, language learners, disabled learners, and people using assistive technology. the sources here do not establish which sequence or classroom activity works best for each population. accessibility and choice should be designed with the people affected, then evaluated rather than assumed.\n", "number": "01"}, {"path": "02-teaching-and-implementation.html", "title": "Teaching and implementation", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# teaching and implementation\n\nthe decision to use a tool starts with a learning goal and the people in the room. unesco’s teacher framework includes human agency, ethics, ai foundations, pedagogy and professional learning. it calls for teacher judgment and meaningful human interaction. it describes competencies; it does not validate a specific product or training course. [s03](07-source-records.md#s03)\n\n## design around the learning task\n\na teacher might invite an initial attempt, then offer a hint, comparison, feedback or an alternative explanation, and finally ask a learner to explain or apply the idea. a learner using assistive technology may need a different workflow. these are design hypotheses; the research does not prove that any single sequence is best. compare learning outcomes, not just completion speed or satisfaction. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01) [s08](07-source-records.md#s08)\n\n## two positive studies, different comparisons\n\nthe harvard study compared a purpose-built, content-grounded tutor used at home with in-class active learning over two topics. the paper reports immediate post-test advantages for that tutor. the conditions differ in environment and mode as well as tool; the result does not isolate a general effect of ai or assess durable learning. [s08](07-source-records.md#s08)\n\nthe nigerian program paired structured after-school sessions, teacher support and ai tools. students who volunteered were randomized to the program or usual instruction. results favor the program on the measured assessment, but substantial and unequal endline completion limits confidence, and no equal-time alternative isolates the tool. [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n\n## institutional practice\n\nbefore implementation, educators can document purpose, alternatives, accessibility, privacy and review arrangements; test materials for accuracy and bias; explain the intended role of the tool; and define how concerns are reported. preserve human oversight for grading and consequential decisions. these are proposed practices informed by guidance, not a substitute for local law or a validated checklist. [s03](07-source-records.md#s03) [s04](07-source-records.md#s04)\n", "number": "02"}, {"path": "03-learning-and-independence.html", "title": "Learning and independence", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "independence", "text": "# learning and independence\n\na finished task and a learned skill are different outcomes. in the turkish mathematics study, standard ai assistance improved practice performance but was followed by lower performance on the same session’s unassisted exam. a specially constructed tutor with teacher-provided solutions and hints had a different result. this is a warning about a specific design and outcome, not proof that ai inevitably weakens thinking. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n\n## a thoughtful pause\n\nbefore using a tool, ask: what am i trying to learn? what can i attempt first? what help would move my thinking forward? how will i check an answer? what would i be able to explain to another person? a learner may choose a hint over a complete solution, work with a peer, or return to a book. these are suggestions rather than rules, and reasonable accommodations can change what independence looks like.\n\n## when to step away\n\nconsider a pause when the activity calls for independent practice, when answers cannot be checked, when the interaction becomes frustrating or distracting, or when private information would otherwise be shared. for sensitive personal concerns, speak with an appropriate trusted person. there is no evidence here for a universal time limit or an obligation to avoid ai altogether.\n\n## check the outcome, not just the impression\n\nthe turkish researchers observed a mismatch between students’ perceptions and exam outcomes. an independent explanation, later application or appropriately accessible assessment can offer more information than confidence alone. these checks should be proportionate, support learners rather than punish them, and be compared with alternatives. long-term effects remain open. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n", "number": "03"}, {"path": "04-children-and-safeguards.html", "title": "Children and safeguards", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "children", "text": "# children and safeguards\n\nchildren are not a single age group. development, disability, context, family circumstances and the system itself affect what is appropriate. unesco calls for age-appropriate educational design and privacy safeguards. unicef’s 2025 guidance sets out ten child-centred requirements including safety, data protection, fairness, accountability, development and inclusion. these are policy recommendations, not proof that a particular deployment is safe. [s04](07-source-records.md#s04) [s05](07-source-records.md#s05)\n\n## practical questions for adults and institutions\n\nbefore use, check current product age requirements and applicable local rules; examine what personal information is requested, retained or shared; consider whether meaningful adult support and an accessible alternative are available; and establish a way to report harmful or misleading outputs. these are dux proposals derived from rights-based guidance. they do not replace a product assessment or legal review.\n\nteach children that generated content can be mistaken, that they can stop an interaction, and that a trusted person can help. avoid requiring sensitive personal disclosures to use an educational activity. in a classroom, educators should select and review activities appropriate to the learner and remain accountable for their use.\n\n## conversational systems and relationships\n\nunicef’s 2026 policy brief identifies distinct child-rights concerns around chatbots and companion-like systems and discusses preventive oversight. its landing page does not quantify how often harms occur. a conversational tool should not be presented as a substitute for trusted people or professional support. [s06](07-source-records.md#s06)\n\n## what remains unknown\n\nthis library does not establish a safe age, exposure duration, or proven universal safeguard. evidence for younger children, long-term outcomes, disabled children and culturally diverse settings needs particular attention. appropriate local safeguards may differ as systems and laws change.\n", "number": "04"}, {"path": "05-access-equity-adults.html", "title": "Access, equity and adult learning", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "access", "text": "# access, equity and adult learning\n\nlearning continues outside schools: at work, at home, in community programs and throughout adulthood. a tool’s potential value depends on affordability, connectivity, language, accessibility and the time and support available to use it. a learner should have meaningful alternatives when a particular tool is inaccessible or inappropriate. these are dux design commitments, not measured impacts of a particular product. [s02](07-source-records.md#s02) [s03](07-source-records.md#s03)\n\n## access is more than availability\n\nproviding an account does not ensure a tool works with assistive technology, provides suitable explanations, respects privacy or supports a learner’s language. evaluate real workflows with the people who use them. avoid treating no-ai practice as a rigid requirement when a tool is part of an accessibility accommodation. review both benefits and risks of the actual configuration.\n\n## adult learners and transfer\n\nthe harvard physics experiment supplies evidence from an undergraduate course, not all adult education. its participants and course content may differ from workplace training, community education or independent study. the study measured immediate post-tests; it cannot establish durable transfer to other tasks. [s08](07-source-records.md#s08)\n\nthe research base collected here leaves significant gaps in older adult learners, adult basic education, disability, multilingual contexts, and low-connectivity settings. future additions should seek primary studies with explicit comparators and document missing or conflicting findings. [s07](07-source-records.md#s07) [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n", "number": "05"}, {"path": "06-method-and-questions.html", "title": "Research method and open questions", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "evidence", "text": "# research method and open questions\n\n## scope and selection\n\nsnapshot: 12 november 2026. this is a targeted collection of primary research and official guidance about ai literacy, education, independent judgment and child safeguards. search focused on unesco, unicef, journals and study authors. it is not a systematic review: there is no exhaustive search strategy, formal screening flow or risk-of-bias meta-analysis. relevant contradictory evidence may be missing.\n\nsource notes identify the source version, design, population or non-study status, accessible material, claims and limitations. we link to primary materials instead of copying third-party articles. publisher access failure is not evidence that a document or a result does not exist. policy recommendations, product claims, experimental observations and dux design suggestions have different evidentiary status.\n\n## questions for the next research round\n\n1. what teaching approaches improve calibrated judgment over time, and for whom?\n2. when do scaffolds improve later transfer rather than performance only while a tool is available?\n3. how do results compare with equal-time teacher-led or peer learning, and what do learners value?\n4. how do accessibility tools change the meaning of independent work and assessment?\n5. what safeguards can be evaluated with children and caregivers without exposing them to avoidable harm?\n6. how do outcomes vary by language, prior knowledge, connectivity, disability, age and socioeconomic context?\n7. how should institutions detect and correct inaccuracies, privacy harms or inequitable outcomes after deployment?\n\n## maintenance\n\nrecheck volatile policies and product terms at use. record source version, date, access and correction history before changing claims. preserve material conflicts. confirm source links and exact denominators, review a representative range of evidence including adverse results, and assess accessibility before publishing. [source records](07-source-records.md)\n", "number": "06"}, {"path": "07-source-records.html", "title": "Annotated source records", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "evidence", "text": "# annotated source records\n\nresearch snapshot: 12 november 2026. source access describes the material actually inspected, not an assertion that unavailable material is absent. counts refer to the units named.\n\n## evidence records\n\n<a id=\"s01\"></a>\n\n### s01 — learning performance and unassisted assessment\n\n**source:** hamsa bastani, osbert bastani, alp sungu, haosen ge, özge kabakcı and rei mariman, “generative ai without guardrails can harm learning: evidence from high school mathematics,” *pnas* 122(26), e2422633122, 25 june 2025, [doi:10.1073/pnas.2422633122](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2422633122). the [20 august 2025 correction](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2518204122) was inspected: it corrects osbert bastani’s affiliation after a production error; it reports no changes to findings.\n\n**access and design:** publisher article and the relevant sections of the [author-hosted manuscript and appendix](https://hamsabastani.github.io/education_llm.pdf) inspected. the publisher-hosted supplement returned http 403; equivalence to the author-hosted version was not independently established. the preregistration itself was not inspected. preregistered classroom-assigned field experiment at one turkish high school in fall 2023, grades 9–11. four 90-minute sessions; approximately 50 classes and nearly 1,000 real students, not model-generated participants. the author-hosted appendix table 3 reports 839 students in the main survey sample: 277 gpt tutor, 320 control, and 242 gpt base; its survey sample including honors contains 943 students. the author version says the main sample excludes honors classes and nonrespondents to the baseline survey. these counts should not be described as total enrollment. the publisher main table 1 reports 2,848 student-session observations per outcome, not 2,848 people. exact enrollment and class counts remain unverified. unit of assignment: classroom; student-session performance analyzed, with classroom-clustered standard errors. honors classes excluded from main sample. students worked from teacher-provided problems, with notes/textbooks alone, a gpt-4 chat interface, or a teacher-informed gpt-4 tutor including solutions and hints, then took an unassisted exam.\n\n**finding:** relative to controls, practice scores were higher with both ai variants; the standard interface group scored 17% lower on the subsequent unassisted exam relative to controls. the guarded tutor group did not show a positive exam effect relative to controls, and the negative effect was largely mitigated. these are short-term results for this setting and these specific tools; they do not establish long-term outcomes, effects on all ages, or that every guardrail works. mechanism analyses suggest copying or reduced engagement, but do not definitively isolate the mechanism. see [publisher methods and results](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2422633122).\n\n<a id=\"s02\"></a>\n\n### s02 — student ai competencies\n\n**source:** fengchun miao, kelly shiohira and natalie lao, unesco, *ai competency framework for students* (2024), [official publication page](https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/ai-competency-framework-students?hub=66682); [full framework, doi 10.54675/jkjb9835](https://doi.org/10.54675/jkjb9835).\n\n**access and design:** official overview and parts of framework pdf inspected. normative curriculum framework, not a trial or a participant sample. twelve competencies span human-centred mindset, ethics, ai techniques and applications, and system design, across understand, apply and create. it recommends agency and critical judgment. this specifies educational goals; it does not show that any particular curriculum causes improved learning.\n\n<a id=\"s03\"></a>\n\n### s03 — educator competencies\n\n**source:** unesco, *ai competency framework for teachers* (2024), [official framework pdf](https://unesdoc.unesco.org/in/rest/annotationsvc/downloadwatermarkedattachment/attach_import_d418160e-3eb2-4d1c-8239-2744911cc721?_=391104eng.pdf&from=1&to=52).\n\n**access and design:** selected pdf sections inspected; normative professional-development framework, not a controlled outcome study. it emphasizes teacher agency, privacy, critical review, human interaction and learner control. its recommendation to test pedagogical strategies is a program-design principle, not proof of efficacy.\n\n<a id=\"s04\"></a>\n\n### s04 — age-appropriate implementation\n\n**source:** unesco, *guidance for generative ai in education and research*, 7 september 2023, [official overview](https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/guidance-generative-ai-education-and-research?hub=67098).\n\n**access and design:** official overview inspected; full guidance not fully reviewed. policy and pedagogical guidance, not a trial. it calls for privacy protections, age limits for independent conversations, and age-appropriate validation and design. specific age thresholds and current jurisdiction-specific legal requirements have not been verified here; avoid presenting any recommendation as a universal law.\n\n<a id=\"s05\"></a>\n\n### s05 — children's rights and safeguards\n\n**source:** unicef innocenti, *guidance on ai and children*, version 3.0, december 2025, [official report page](https://www.unicef.org/innocenti/reports/policy-guidance-ai-children).\n\n**access and design:** issuer's overview inspected; complete pdf not reviewed. rights-based guidance informed by consultation and existing evidence, including a twelve-country study; that number is countries, not a participant count. the overview lists safety, privacy, fairness, transparency, accountability, development, inclusion and children's ai skills among ten requirements. these are recommendations, not evidence that a particular safeguard has been validated or legal advice.\n\n<a id=\"s06\"></a>\n\n### s06 — conversational ai and children\n\n**source:** unicef, *when ai becomes a friend: child rights risks, harms, and regulatory responses to ai chatbots and companions*, june 2026, [official document page](https://www.unicef.org/documents/when-ai-becomes-friend-child-rights-risks).\n\n**access and design:** issuer overview inspected, not full policy brief. policy analysis compares approaches across six jurisdictions as of may 2026; six is a jurisdiction count, not children or experiments. it identifies risks and recommends preventive safeguards and accountability. the overview does not quantify risk or establish prevalence.\n\n<a id=\"s07\"></a>\n\n### s07 — a short literacy intervention\n\n**source:** brett puppart and jaan aru, *a brief ai literacy intervention does not significantly reduce over-reliance and increases under-reliance on chatgpt: a randomized study*, [arxiv:2503.10556v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.10556v2), revised 18 september 2026; originally submitted 13 march 2025 under a different title. doi [10.48550/arxiv.2503.10556](https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2503.10556).\n\n**access and design:** selected methods, results and limitations of the 28-page [v2 pdf](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.10556v2) inspected, as well as the version history. preprint; peer-review or subsequent publication status not established. in october 2024 researchers invited 72 estonian schools; 10 forwarded invitations. forty-two real 12th-grade students completed the questionnaire; six were excluded for stated speed criteria, leaving 36 analyzed, ages 18–19: 19 intervention and 17 control. individuals were randomized. each saw eight selected math puzzles and fixed screenshots of model recommendations, four correct and four incorrect, and decided whether to adopt them; they did not interact live with the model. the control read a general introduction and the intervention received additional information. participant and trial are distinct units.\n\n**finding and limitations:** across participants, the reported mean proportion of incorrect recommendations adopted was 52.1%. the intervention group’s mean was 48.68% versus 55.88% for controls; the difference was not statistically significant (p = .471), which does not establish equivalence or no effect. rejection of correct advice was higher in the intervention group (36.84% versus 19.12%; p = .025). the small sample, selection, exclusion criteria, fixed screenshots and short task constrain precision and generalization; the study does not establish that ai literacy education generally fails, or what happens in ordinary iterative chatbot use.\n\n\n<a id=\"s08\"></a>\n\n### s08 — undergraduate physics tutor trial\n\n**source:** greg kestin, kelly miller, anna klales, timothy milbourne and gregorio ponti, “ai tutoring outperforms in-class active learning: an rct introducing a novel research-based design in an authentic educational setting,” *scientific reports* 15, 17458, 3 june 2025. [doi:10.1038/s41598-025-97652-6](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-97652-6).\n\n**access and design:** publisher article methods and results inspected; supplement not inspected. two-week crossover randomized experiment in one harvard undergraduate introductory physics course, fall 2023. of 233 enrolled students, 194 met inclusion criteria involving consent, participation in both modes and completed tests. peer-instruction groups of 2–3 were assigned together. the study compared an ai tutor used at home with in-class active learning on two topics; its tutor used pre-written answers and content-specific prompting. the reported post-test counts are 142 ai-condition and 174 classroom-condition lesson observations, not distinct people.\n\n**finding and limits:** the paper reports higher immediate post-test performance in the tutor condition. the environments, delivery and conditions differ together; this does not isolate ai from all other differences, measure long-term retention, establish effects in other learners or show that ai generally replaces teachers.\n\n<a id=\"s09\"></a>\n\n### s09 — nigeria after-school pilot\n\n**source:** martin elias de simone, federico hernan tiberti, maria barron rodriguez, federico manolio, wuraola mosuro and eliot jolomi dikoru, *from chalkboards to chatbots: evaluating the impact of generative ai on learning outcomes in nigeria*, world bank education global department working paper, may 2025. [working paper pdf](https://thedocs.worldbank.org/en/doc/0c08ef70ff0e1d81997f116923c0e2c7-0140022025/related/edu-wp-18-evaluating-the-impact-of-generative-ai-on-learning-outcomes-in-nigeria.pdf); [reproducibility package doi](https://doi.org/10.60572/cre7-j348).\n\n**access and design:** working paper methods and results and reproducibility-package overview inspected; data and code not independently rerun. interested first-year senior secondary students in benin city, nigeria, were individually randomized: 657 to a six-week teacher-supported after-school ai program and 671 to usual schooling. the endline assessment included 422 treatment and 337 control students, 759 total; these are people, not sessions. the program combined materials, adult support and gpt-4-based copilot. the control did not receive an equal-time alternative.\n\n**finding and limits:** the authors report a 0.31 standard-deviation improvement in a combined english, ai-knowledge and digital-skills assessment and 0.238 standard deviations in english; the relevant regression reports 654 observations, fewer than the 759 endline completers. differential attrition was significant; the paper reports sensitivity analyses, but selection remains a limitation. the bundled intervention does not establish the separate effect of ai, nor its long-term impact. the working paper’s projected earnings are projections, not observed participant outcomes.\n\n<a id=\"s10\"></a>\n\n### s10 — medical education observational study\n\n**source:** thomas thesen and soo hwan park, “a generative ai teaching assistant for personalized learning in medical education,” *npj digital medicine* 8, 627, 4 november 2025. [doi:10.1038/s41746-025-02022-1](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41746-025-02022-1).\n\n**access and design:** publisher methods, selected results and limitations inspected. observational deployment of a course-material-grounded assistant across two medical-school cohorts, not a randomized learning-outcome trial. among survey respondents, 22 of 56 in the first cohort and 23 of 87 in the second reported at least one use. the paper reports 328 conversations in thematic analysis (216 / 65.9% in its first topic category); conversations are not distinct participants, and topic codes can overlap. gpt-4o assisted thematic coding; a 15% subset received human validation. the authors did not systematically assess answer accuracy or track individual engagement patterns. findings describe usage and reported experience in this setting; they do not establish learning gains or that retrieval eliminates hallucination.\n\n<a id=\"s11\"></a>\n\n### s11 — evaluating education-technology privacy\n\n**source:** u.s. department of education, student privacy policy office, [privacy and education technology](https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/privacy-and-education-technology), webpage, publication date not established; accessed 12 november 2026.\n\n**access and design:** agency overview inspected, linked model terms and underlying legal guidance not individually reviewed. the page links resources on assessing how online services collect, use and transmit information. this is a us agency resource, not a study or a complete statement of requirements in every jurisdiction. laws and interpretations can change.\n\n<a id=\"s12\"></a>\n\n### s12 — child privacy and schools\n\n**source:** u.s. federal trade commission, [complying with coppa: frequently asked questions](https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/complying-coppa-frequently-asked-questions), section n, webpage accessed 12 november 2026.\n\n**access and design:** selected agency faq on schools inspected; not a trial. it describes limited circumstances in which a school may act for a parent in relation to educational services, and distinguishes school-benefit uses from other commercial purposes. the detailed requirements and applicable law depend on context and may change. dux does not provide legal advice or assert that this us guidance applies elsewhere.\n\n<a id=\"s13\"></a>\n\n### s13 — ai risk management framework\n\n**source:** elham tabassi, national institute of standards and technology, *artificial intelligence risk management framework (ai rmf 1.0)*, 26 january 2023, [doi:10.6028/nist.ai.100-1](https://doi.org/10.6028/nist.ai.100-1); [core functions](https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/airmf/5-sec-core/).\n\n**access and design:** nist publication overview and online core inspected. voluntary, non-sector-specific governance framework, not a school efficacy study or a binding universal standard. it organizes activities around govern, map, measure and manage, and says the functions are not a checklist. nist notes a revision is in progress; check current status before use.\n\n<a id=\"s14\"></a>\n\n### s14 — web accessibility guidance\n\n**source:** world wide web consortium, *web content accessibility guidelines 2.2*, [w3c recommendation, 12 december 2024](https://www.w3.org/tr/2024/rec-wcag22-20241212/).\n\n**access and design:** recommendation overview and publication history inspected. technical accessibility standard, not an empirical evaluation of an ai tutor. w3c states its guidelines do not address every need. conformance of any dux or third-party service has not been established by inclusion here.\n\n<a id=\"s15\"></a>\n\n### s15 — education technology in context\n\n**source:** unesco global education monitoring report team, *technology in education: a tool on whose terms?*, 2023, [official publication page](https://www.unesco.org/gem-report/en/publication/technology?hub=83250).\n\n**access and design:** issuer’s overview and selected summary text inspected, not every underlying study. the report considers education technology through relevance, equity, scalability and sustainability. its broader education-technology scope must not be recast as a trial of generative ai. it advocates learning centred on people and attention to potential adverse consequences.\n\n<a id=\"s16\"></a>\n\n### s16 — teacher ai program in peru\n\n**source:** carla z. glave, carolina lopez, ezequiel molina and román andrés zárate, *not faster, but better: generative ai and teacher skill in peru 2025*, world bank dataset description, 20 july 2026, [doi:10.48529/t6x6-2y70](https://doi.org/10.48529/t6x6-2y70).\n\n**access and design:** primary data catalogue description and abstract inspected; full paper, dataset and preregistration not inspected. the record describes 1,269 sixth-grade teachers across 390 public schools in metropolitan lima, with school-network eligibility and school-level randomization. it identifies a program combining training, a copilot licence and follow-up, with further randomized message features. the catalogue abstract reports greater adoption without measured time savings on core tasks; this is a bundled program claim, not evidence on student learning or on ai software in isolation. the catalogue is a research lead: outcome measures, attrition, uncertainty and paper version require full-paper verification before stronger claims.\n\n**update, 12 november 2026:** the [october 2026 working paper](https://documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/099309210012625906/pdf/idu-c261aadb-6c32-48af-a07c-9cff5e86c716.pdf), policy research working paper 11476, doi [10.1596/1813-9450-11476](https://doi.org/10.1596/1813-9450-11476), is now accessible; selected methods, results and tables were inspected. of 390 schools, 196 (660 teachers) were assigned the package and 194 (609 teachers) control. controls retained access to publicly available ai. the authors report higher scores on prompt rubrics and better judgments of weak ai output, and no significant time reduction across seven self-reported tasks. time analysis table 3 lists 607 observations; other analyses have different denominators. the authors report no differential endline response, which does not remove all selection concerns. student learning was not established. dataset and code were not rerun; the research was funded in part by the microsoft ai economy institute. the earlier catalogue-only access note is retained as the record of the initial review.\n\n\n<a id=\"s17\"></a>\n\n### s17 — undergraduate preparation and belonging\n\n**source:** renlin wang, jian sun and shu wen, “structured generative ai preparation and classroom belonging in a six-class randomized trial,” *scientific reports*, accepted article published online 26 october 2026, [doi:10.1038/s41598-026-78039-7](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-026-78039-7).\n\n**access and design:** publisher abstract inspected; complete methods, data and supplement not independently reviewed. the publisher labels it an accepted version subject to further editing. six intact classes at one chinese university were randomized, three sections and 110 students per condition. the intervention combines ai exploration, confidence reflection, question generation and instructor responsiveness. it measures classroom belonging and related reported outcomes; the section is the assignment unit, and the number of sections is six, not 220 independent randomized units.\n\n**finding and limits:** the abstract reports a difference in belonging at week 12 under its section-level analysis, while its exact two-sided randomization tests give p = .100 for the primary contrasts. the complex intervention does not isolate ai or establish effects in other settings. this is a limited-access research lead pending full methods review, not a broad claim that ai improves belonging.\n\n\n<a id=\"s18\"></a>\n\n### s18 — oecd and european commission ai literacy framework\n\n**source:** oecd and european commission, *empowering learners for the age of ai: an ai literacy framework for primary and secondary education*, 18 june 2026, [doi:10.1787/65cd27d4-en](https://doi.org/10.1787/65cd27d4-en).\n\n**access and design:** official overview and abstract inspected; complete 64-page framework not fully reviewed. normative framework for desired learning outcomes; no participant count or intervention effect. it includes understanding, critical evaluation and ethical and creative use. it complements, rather than experimentally validates, unesco’s framework. specific activities and age progression require review of the full report.\n\n<a id=\"s19\"></a>\n\n### s19 — ai support for human tutors\n\n**source:** rose e. wang, ana t. ribeiro, carly d. robinson, susanna loeb and dora demszky, *tutor copilot: a human-ai approach for scaling real-time expertise*, [arxiv:2410.03017](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.03017), 3 october 2024 version inspected. peer-review and subsequent version status not established.\n\n**access and design:** preprint methods and selected results inspected; preregistration and data not independently checked. two-month study in a southern us district, nine schools and grades 3–8. the district identified 1,787 students; 900 tutors were initially randomized, 450 per arm; at study launch 782 remained, 386 treatment and 396 control. the text also reports 874 full-time tutors assigned to schools, a separate figure whose relationship to the launch sample is not fully clarified here. randomization was by tutor, not student. final analysis comprises 4,136 sessions and over 550,000 chat messages, neither being a count of individuals. intervention tutors could request ai-generated suggestions; control tutors taught without it. the lead author worked part-time with the provider.\n\n**finding and limits:** the paper reports exit-ticket pass rate of 62% control versus 66% treatment, a four percentage-point intention-to-treat difference. it does not establish lasting achievement, effects in other settings, or safety of unsupervised child-facing chatbots. tutors reported some suggestions were not grade appropriate. classifier-based analysis of chat language and tutor interviews are different kinds of evidence from the exit-ticket outcome.\n\n**update, 12 november 2026:** a later [november 2025 working-paper version](https://edworkingpapers.com/sites/default/files/ai24_1054_v2.pdf) was located and selected methods and limitations inspected. it reports 900 tutors initially assigned; 783 with study sessions (388 treatment, 395 control), 1,534 students initially eligible and 1,013 attending at least one study session, in grades 3–6. these differ from the 2024 version’s figures above; do not merge versions or silently substitute counts. in the newer version students could be matched with different tutors across sessions. it reports the same direction for the session-level exit-ticket outcome; the authors report no statistically significant end-of-year math effect and caution that their tutor-randomized design cannot directly estimate a student-level treatment effect on that outcome. the later version’s different sample definitions and coding figures require version-specific citation.\n\n<a id=\"s20\"></a>\n\n### s20 — prompting instruction in introductory computer science\n\n**source:** ruiwei xiao, runlong ye, xinying hou, jessica wen, harsh kumar, michael liut and john stamper, *transforming genai policy to prompting instruction: an rct of scalable prompting interventions in a cs1 course*, [arxiv:2602.16033](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.16033), 17 february 2026 version inspected. preprint; peer-review or later version not established.\n\n**access and design:** preprint methods and selected results inspected. at one north american university, 979 enrolled students were randomized to four instruction conditions; 431 with valid responses across all phases formed the complete-case sample (102, 112, 109 and 108 respectively). this 44% complete-case rate limits interpretation. students generated prompts assessed against the researchers’ rubric; the most active condition included gpt-4o feedback. the authors report gains in the measured prompting task and no direct final-exam differences between conditions. the relationship between prompting gains and exam scores is observational within the study and does not establish that prompting practice caused higher course performance.\n\n<a id=\"s21\"></a>\n\n### s21 — structured tutoring and modality in an online mba\n\n**source:** shihao yang, marshall van alstyne and chrysanthos dellarocas, *when ai tutors speak: evidence from a randomized field experiment*, [arxiv:2609.23958](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.23958), working draft dated 20 september 2026. preprint; peer review not established.\n\n**access and design:** methods and selected results of the draft inspected. in one online mba finance course, 86 consenting students were randomized to a structured course-grounded tutor or a holdout that could use consumer ai. fifty-one completed both study assessment waves; the paper also reports instructor gradebook outcomes for all 86. within the tutor arm, modality alternated weekly. the paper reports 32 students in the within-student voice/text comparison. students, assessment waves, weekly interactions and course examinations are distinct units.\n\n**finding and limits:** the researchers report an advantage for tutor access on their assessment and a smaller same-direction difference on the instructor’s final. attrition affects the custom-assessment analysis; the final was not itself a preregistered outcome. no general conclusion about all adults or all voice tutors follows, and a non-significant modality difference does not by itself prove equivalence. the tutor, subject, cohort and short field period bound the findings.\n\n<a id=\"s22\"></a>\n\n### s22 — human engagement support for elementary reading\n\n**source:** carly d. robinson, david gormley, ana trindade ribeiro and susanna loeb, *access is not enough: human support improves engagement with ai tutoring*, edworkingpaper 26-1451, june 2026, [doi:10.26300/pz7p-p388](https://doi.org/10.26300/pz7p-p388); [author-hosted paper](https://scale.stanford.edu/sites/default/files/ai26-1451.pdf).\n\n**access and design:** working-paper methods and selected results inspected; preregistration and underlying data not independently checked. two individually randomized trials compared an ai reading platform with the same platform plus in-person engagement support. district a assigned 84 treatment and 90 control students in grades 1–5; district b assigned 36 treatment and 145 control students in grades 1–3, 355 children total. the human role focused on engagement and troubleshooting, not direct reading instruction; scheduled intervention structures differed. the study is not a comparison of ai tutoring with human instruction or no tutoring.\n\n**finding and limits:** the paper reports roughly one additional minute per week of platform use in district a and 4.4 in district b, with low usage overall. exploratory reading achievement outcomes were not significantly different; this does not prove no effect. districts, measures and implementation varied, and the provider’s recommended usage level is a provider claim, not an established universal threshold.\n", "number": "07"}, {"path": "08-assessment.html", "title": "Assessment in an AI-rich setting", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "assessment", "text": "# assessment in an ai-rich setting\n\nassessment asks what a learner understands, can do and can explain. the answer may differ when a tool is available from when it is not. the turkish mathematics study illustrates that distinction on immediate post-practice exams, but does not specify a universal assessment policy. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n\n## choose the construct first\n\na learner may need to demonstrate independent arithmetic, evaluate a source, revise a draft, collaborate or use an accessibility tool. these are different constructs. an assessment that bans every aid may exclude a learner without measuring the intended ability; a task that permits automatic solutions may not show whether a foundational skill was acquired. teachers should decide what they want to learn about the learner, and design an accessible assessment accordingly. this is an educational design proposal, not a trial result.\n\n## evidence in context\n\nthe undergraduate physics study used pre- and immediate post-tests after two short lessons. the nigerian study used a paper assessment after a six-week program and had unequal follow-up completion. neither should be read as proof of long-term transfer. [s08](07-source-records.md#s08) [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n\n## possible approaches to evaluate\n\nuse an explanation or short conversation alongside a finished product; compare an initial attempt with a revision; ask a learner to check a deliberately flawed answer; or offer an appropriately accessible independent task. assess the approach for fairness, workload and whether it measures the stated goal. do not infer misconduct solely from a detector or an ai-like writing style; the sources here do not establish a reliable detection method.\n", "number": "08"}, {"path": "09-privacy-accountability.html", "title": "Privacy, data and accountability", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "safety", "text": "# privacy, data and accountability\n\neducational tools may handle prompts, student work, usage logs, account information and sensitive context. institutions should understand the actual service and its data flows before adopting it. a model answer can also contain invented personal or factual information. adult oversight does not by itself erase these risks. [s04](07-source-records.md#s04) [s05](07-source-records.md#s05)\n\n## questions to ask a provider\n\n- what information is necessary for the learning activity, and what can be omitted?\n- who can access it, where is it stored, and how long is it retained?\n- can it be used for advertising, training or other purposes? what choices actually exist?\n- how can a person correct or remove information, report an incident, or leave the service?\n- what happens if the service changes its terms or is unavailable?\n\nthese are due-diligence questions, not proof that any answer is sufficient. the us department of education links a model terms checklist for evaluating collection, use and transmission. its page is a us resource and does not settle legal requirements for every organization. [s11](07-source-records.md#s11)\n\n## children and school agreements\n\nthe ftc’s school faq describes specific conditions for school consent in the us and distinguishes school-benefit uses from other commercial purposes. whether and how an institution may use a service depends on current law, age, service design and agreements. seek appropriate local advice; do not treat this overview as a legal checklist. [s12](07-source-records.md#s12)\n\n## human responsibility\n\nestablish who approves a tool, who reviews its output, how learners can contest a decision, and how to suspend a use that is causing harm. human review should have the time, authority and information to change an outcome. these are proposed governance practices grounded in institutional guidance, not verified results of a particular program. [s03](07-source-records.md#s03) [s05](07-source-records.md#s05)\n", "number": "09"}, {"path": "10-tool-evaluation.html", "title": "Evaluating an educational AI tool", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# evaluating an educational ai tool\n\nno single feature label proves a tool is safe, accurate or educationally effective. evaluate a particular product, version and use case against a specific learning goal and a realistic alternative. a system designed to offer hints may still give answers or make mistakes. a system grounded in course documents can cite materials and still require independent accuracy checks. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01) [s10](07-source-records.md#s10)\n\n## before a pilot\n\ndescribe the learners, setting, expected benefits, accessibility needs, human supports, data handling and acceptable alternatives. ask whether the same problem could be addressed by existing teaching, peers, books or an assistive tool. define how learners and educators can decline, report problems or stop. these steps are dux proposals to be adapted and evaluated.\n\n## during a pilot\n\nobserve whether the tool provides the help intended, whether learners can challenge or leave it, and whether errors reach students. invite feedback without making participation coercive. measure learning and accessibility as well as use and satisfaction. monitor outcomes across groups only with appropriate privacy safeguards and sufficient sample sizes.\n\n## after a pilot\n\ncompare results with the stated goal and an appropriate alternative, including a later assessment where feasible. consider the workload, cost, data risks and who was unable to participate. report missing outcomes and uncertainty. the nigerian intervention bundled ai access, teaching and extra time; its measured program effect cannot identify which component caused the result. [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n\nthe medical-school deployment observed usage and feedback, but did not systematically measure answer accuracy or establish improved learning. its own authors note constraints on interpretation. [s10](07-source-records.md#s10)\n", "number": "10"}, {"path": "11-learning-activities.html", "title": "Learning activities to try and evaluate", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "literacy", "text": "# learning activities to try and evaluate\n\nthese activities are examples, not validated lesson plans. adapt them to learner age, disability, language, goals, resources and local policies. participation in an ai activity should not be the only route to the learning objective.\n\n## compare, check, explain\n\ngive a learner two short explanations of a topic, one generated and one from a suitable human-authored source. ask what is supported, what is missing, how they would check a claim and what remains uncertain. use examples appropriate to the learner; provide sources and human help. the aim is calibrated evaluation, not automatic distrust. the small short-text study shows that explaining limitations alone did not significantly change over-reliance on its task. [s07](07-source-records.md#s07)\n\n## try, seek a hint, return\n\ninvite an initial attempt, then a hint or conversation with a teacher, peer, book or tool. ask the learner to revisit the original problem and explain the decision. offer needed assistive supports. a later, accessible transfer task can help evaluate whether the activity supported learning. the turkish study motivates testing scaffold design, but does not prove this activity works. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n\n## design an ai-free path\n\ninvite learners to choose a period for reading, making, discussing or practicing without a generative tool when that fits the objective. reflect afterward on what was easy, difficult or worth checking. this is an option, not a moral rule or an instruction to abandon accessibility support. evidence in this collection does not establish a universal time threshold.\n\n## for educators and families\n\nuse a fictitious example to discuss personal information, consent and the option to stop; identify a trusted person to ask for help. adults can review the tool and its rules in advance. these are safety-oriented suggestions informed by child-rights guidance, not evidence of their standalone effectiveness. [s05](07-source-records.md#s05)\n", "number": "11"}, {"path": "12-study-comparisons.html", "title": "Comparing three studies", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "evidence", "text": "# comparing three studies\n\nthe studies below are useful because they do not deliver one simple answer. each evaluates a different package of support, population, comparison and outcome. none should be used as a ranking of people or a prediction for a particular learner.\n\n## high-school mathematics in türkiye\n\na classroom-assigned study compared textbook-and-notes practice with two gpt-4 designs, then used unassisted exams within each session. the general interface improved practice but reduced the subsequent exam score relative to control; the teacher-informed tutor largely mitigated the loss. one school and short-term outcomes constrain generalization. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n\n## undergraduate physics in the united states\n\na crossover study compared a purpose-built ai tutor used at home with active learning in class. immediate post-test outcomes favored the tutor under the study conditions. the comparison changes mode and setting together, and does not establish durable effects. [s08](07-source-records.md#s08)\n\n## after-school learning in nigeria\n\na randomized, six-week program offered structured content, teacher support and ai to interested students, compared with regular instruction. the program improved measured scores in the reported analysis; endline attrition differed by group. the design does not separate ai from extra time and other support. [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n\n## what to ask next\n\ntest similar tools against equal-time alternatives, measure delayed learning and transfer, include accessible and diverse settings, report missing data, and examine possible harms. better task performance, user confidence, and learning are distinct outcomes. this is a research agenda, not a result already demonstrated.\n", "number": "12"}, {"path": "13-accessibility-and-participation.html", "title": "Accessibility and participation", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "access", "text": "# accessibility and participation\n\naccessibility is part of the learning goal, not a final decoration. people may use screen readers, captions, alternative input, adjusted presentation, translated materials or assistive systems. the right combination depends on the individual and activity. a proposed ai tool should be assessed with disabled learners and the assistive technologies they actually use.\n\n## separate participation from a particular tool\n\noffer accessible ways to read, respond, ask questions and seek help. an ai-free option should not remove an accommodation someone needs; an ai-based option should not require a person to disclose disability or private information unnecessarily. these are dux design proposals, not evidence of effectiveness in every setting.\n\n## standards and human review\n\nw3c’s wcag 2.2 provides recommendations for accessible web content and notes that it does not address every need. automated tests can identify some issues but do not replace assessment with people and assistive technologies. a cited standard does not certify an application. [s14](07-source-records.md#s14)\n\n## equity beyond access\n\nask who can afford the tool, whether connectivity is reliable, whether language and examples fit the learner, and whether participation exposes personal data. unesco’s broader education-technology report asks whether a technology is relevant, equitable, scalable and sustainable. its assessment is not a test of a particular generative ai system. [s15](07-source-records.md#s15)\n", "number": "13"}, {"path": "14-governance-lifecycle.html", "title": "Governance across the life of a tool", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "safety", "text": "# governance across the life of a tool\n\na decision to introduce ai in education does not end at procurement. institutions can define who is accountable, how risks and benefits will be assessed, when a tool will be reviewed, and how people can contest a harmful outcome. nist’s voluntary, general-purpose ai framework organizes its work around govern, map, measure and manage. it is not a school-specific validation or a checklist. [s13](07-source-records.md#s13)\n\n## govern and map\n\nidentify the decision owner, affected learners and educators, purpose, plausible harms and benefits, alternatives, data flows and limits of the proposed use. include people who will use or be affected by the service. map power as well as technology: who can decline, who can contest an outcome, and who carries the work of checking?\n\n## measure and respond\n\ndefine what counts as success and harm, the comparison, acceptable uncertainty and a route for incident reporting. check actual performance in context, including accessibility and output quality. set conditions for pausing, changing or retiring the tool. review when the model, provider, terms or population changes. these are dux proposals inspired by the framework, not proof of compliance with it.\n\n## protect agency\n\nhuman review requires someone with the authority, time and information to disagree with the tool. a nominal approval step without an ability to change the decision may not provide meaningful accountability. unesco’s teacher framework places human agency and responsibility at the centre of educators’ competencies. [s03](07-source-records.md#s03)\n", "number": "14"}, {"path": "15-questions-for-people.html", "title": "Questions for learners, families and educators", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "literacy", "text": "# questions for learners, families and educators\n\nthere is no single right sequence for every learner. these prompts support conversation; they are not an assessment, a validated safety instrument, or a substitute for an educator’s or caregiver’s judgment.\n\n## for a learner\n\nwhat do i hope to understand? can i make a first attempt, alone or with the support i need? would a hint or a person help more than an answer? what evidence would make me change my mind? could i explain my conclusion? when would i choose to pause?\n\n## for a family\n\nwhat is age-appropriate for this child and this service? can we try an activity together? does the child know they can stop and speak to a trusted adult? what information does the service collect and how can we report a problem? unicef’s guidance centres children’s rights, safety and development, rather than offering a universal safe age. [s05](07-source-records.md#s05)\n\n## for an educator\n\nwhat learning objective is served? which students may be excluded? what will remain human-led? how will feedback and generated facts be checked? can students demonstrate their learning through an accessible alternative? how will the approach be evaluated and adjusted? unesco’s teacher framework treats agency and professional learning as core concerns. [s03](07-source-records.md#s03)\n\n## for an institution\n\nwho approves the service, reviews the agreement, manages data and responds when something goes wrong? what other approach could meet the same need? what evidence would cause a pause? are there adequate time and resources for educators to make the process meaningful? [s11](07-source-records.md#s11) [s13](07-source-records.md#s13)\n", "number": "15"}, {"path": "16-glossary.html", "title": "A glossary for the library", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "evidence", "text": "# a glossary for the library\n\n## artificial intelligence\n\na broad term for systems designed to perform tasks associated with prediction, classification, generation or decision support. different systems use different techniques; a generative chatbot is one type, not the whole field.\n\n## generative ai and language models\n\ngenerative systems produce content such as text or images. a language model generates text from patterns learned during training. a fluent response is not itself evidence of factual accuracy, understanding or a reliable source. the design and capabilities of systems change over time.\n\n## ai literacy\n\nthe knowledge, skills and values needed to understand, question and engage with ai. unesco’s student framework includes human agency, ethics, techniques and design; it does not reduce literacy to prompting. [s02](07-source-records.md#s02)\n\n## scaffolding and feedback\n\nsupport that helps a learner work toward a goal, such as a question, hint, example or feedback. the term does not guarantee a benefit: the content and timing of support need evaluation. the two tutors in the turkish study used different prompts and teacher-provided materials. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n\n## retrieval-augmented generation\n\na design that supplies retrieved material to a generative model when composing an answer. relevant source material may make answers easier to inspect, but retrieval does not guarantee correctness. the medical-school deployment did not systematically assess its response accuracy. [s10](07-source-records.md#s10)\n\n## outcome, comparator and unit\n\nan outcome is what a study measures, such as a later test score. a comparator is the alternative against which the intervention is assessed. the unit might be a person, classroom, student-session, conversation or model output. these must not be interchanged. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01) [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n\n## association and causal effect\n\nan association is a relationship observed in data; it need not mean one variable caused the other. randomized comparisons can support causal conclusions about the assigned intervention under their design and assumptions, but missing data, bundled interventions and limits to generalization remain important.\n", "number": "16"}, {"path": "17-teacher-work-and-agency.html", "title": "Teacher work, support and agency", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# teacher work, support and agency\n\nteachers decide what to teach, notice misunderstanding, build relationships, adapt materials and assess learning. tools may assist with particular tasks, but an output is not self-validating and time saved on one activity may be spent elsewhere. unesco’s teacher competency framework emphasises human agency, responsible use, pedagogy and continuing professional learning. [s03](07-source-records.md#s03)\n\n## time, quality and workload\n\na world bank data-catalogue description of a randomized program in peru reports increased adoption but no measured time savings on core teacher tasks. the program included training, a licence and follow-up; its full paper and outcome methods have not yet been examined here. treat this as a research lead, not a settled claim about all teachers or tools. [s16](07-source-records.md#s16)\n\nask whether a proposed use genuinely reduces workload after reviewing, correcting, documenting and explaining outputs. measure quality and equity alongside speed. consult teachers about what work they want supported and what decisions should remain theirs. these are proposed evaluation questions, not established effects.\n\n## preparing and reviewing materials\n\na teacher can use a system to generate alternatives or examples, then check facts, appropriateness, accessibility and representation against trusted materials. they can also choose a non-ai process. a human review step needs time and authority to reject or revise an output. keep personal student data out of a tool unless the specific use has been assessed and authorized. [s03](07-source-records.md#s03) [s11](07-source-records.md#s11)\n\n## professional learning\n\nconsider practical evaluation, privacy, bias, human accountability and pedagogical design, not only prompting. a framework can specify valuable competencies without proving a particular workshop achieves them. evaluate training over time with teachers and learners, and give people an accessible path to raise concerns.\n", "number": "17"}, {"path": "18-community-and-belonging.html", "title": "Relationships, community and belonging", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "children", "text": "# relationships, community and belonging\n\neducation happens between people as well as through materials. a tool may help a learner prepare questions or find an explanation, but educational quality also involves human connection, classroom participation and the ability to seek help. unesco’s broader technology report places learners’ interests and human interaction at the centre. [s15](07-source-records.md#s15)\n\n## what one new study asks\n\nan accepted 2026 paper reports a six-section randomized study in one chinese university of a combined preparation protocol involving ai, reflection, question generation and instructor responsiveness. it reports a section-level belonging difference, but the exact randomization tests and small number of sections warrant care. the abstract has been inspected, not the full methods; the intervention does not isolate the ai component. [s17](07-source-records.md#s17)\n\n## design for connection\n\nas a dux proposal, invite learners to bring unresolved questions to peers or a teacher, reserve time for discussion, and give people a way to ask a human for help. check whether the activity increases or reduces participation for different learners. provide alternatives for people who need different forms of communication.\n\n## children's relationships\n\nunicef has identified distinct child-rights concerns around conversational and companion-like systems. a tool should not be presented as a substitute for trusted people; family and institutional safeguards should be adapted to the child and setting. the guidance is not a measurement of prevalence. [s06](07-source-records.md#s06)\n", "number": "18"}, {"path": "19-comparing-support.html", "title": "Four studies of educational support", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# four studies of educational support\n\na study can test access to a tool, a tutor’s response to suggestions, a training package or a complete learning program. these interventions cannot be treated as interchangeable. each result depends on the comparison, participants, outcome and time frame.\n\n## a tutor supporting a student\n\nin the turkish mathematics experiment, the standard gpt-4 interface increased assisted practice performance but was followed by lower unassisted exam performance than notes and textbooks. the teacher-informed tutor avoided much of that loss but did not show an exam advantage over control. it was one school and a short-term assessment. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01)\n\n## a tool supporting a human tutor\n\nin the us tutor copilot preprint, tutors rather than children were randomized to access suggestions in live mathematics tutoring. the reported exit-ticket pass rate rose from 62% to 66%. tutor attrition, age-inappropriate suggestions, provider involvement and the short-term outcome matter. sessions and chat messages are not students. this does not test replacing human tutors. [s19](07-source-records.md#s19)\n\n## a program for teachers\n\nthe peru trial randomized school-level access to a package of training, a licence and follow-up. its working paper reports improved performance on certain ai-use assessments without measured time savings across seven self-reported tasks. student achievement was not measured as a demonstrated outcome. the package’s components cannot be separated by the main comparison. [s16](07-source-records.md#s16)\n\n## a structured tutor for adults\n\na small online mba preprint compared access to a course-grounded tutor with a holdout where consumer ai remained available. only 51 of 86 randomized students completed both researcher assessments; a separate instructor final was available for all 86. it reports a positive direction, but its setting and assessment choices prevent sweeping claims about adult learning. [s21](07-source-records.md#s21)\n\n## what can be compared\n\nfor each, ask: who was randomized, what changed together, what was measured, how many people completed the outcome, and how long did the effect last? a product demonstration, satisfaction survey, performance with assistance and unassisted retention each answer different questions. [method](06-method-and-questions.md)\n", "number": "19"}, {"path": "20-literacy-across-ages.html", "title": "Building AI literacy across ages", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "literacy", "text": "# building ai literacy across ages\n\nai literacy is not confined to a single application. a person may encounter ai in a search result, a recommendation, a school tool or a workplace process without having chosen a chatbot. the oecd and european commission framework describes knowledge, skills and attitudes for primary and secondary learners, including critical evaluation and responsible use. it is a framework of goals, not an evaluation of a particular lesson. [s18](07-source-records.md#s18)\n\n## begin with a familiar decision\n\na younger learner might examine whether a generated description matches a picture and ask an adult for help. an older learner might compare source claims, discuss whose perspectives are missing, or examine a generated citation. an adult learner might evaluate a tool against professional responsibilities or compare explanations with a reliable reference. these are dux examples, not proven age bands or validated interventions.\n\n## practice judgment in both directions\n\nappropriate reliance involves noticing errors and recognizing when evidence supports a useful answer. the small estonian study found that one short text did not significantly reduce reliance on incorrect screenshots, while participants in the intervention rejected more correct answers. the study does not show that all ai literacy instruction fails. [s07](07-source-records.md#s07)\n\na university preprint randomized students to four forms of instruction about educational prompting and reports measured gains on a prompting rubric. only 431 of 979 randomized course enrollees remained in its complete-case analysis, and there was no direct between-condition difference on the final exam. prompt quality and general academic learning are different outcomes. [s20](07-source-records.md#s20)\n\n## questions for educators\n\nwhat does the learner already know? what can be demonstrated without requiring an account? how will the activity handle privacy and accessibility? can the learner use a non-ai route? what would count as a useful change in judgment after the lesson? learners should have a meaningful opportunity to disagree or stop. [s02](07-source-records.md#s02) [s05](07-source-records.md#s05)\n", "number": "20"}, {"path": "21-adult-learning-evidence.html", "title": "Evidence for adults and lifelong learning", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "adult", "text": "# evidence for adults and lifelong learning\n\nadult learning includes formal university study, professional training, family learning and self-directed curiosity. these are different settings with different constraints. the current dux corpus contains several adult examples but does not represent all adult learners, occupations, languages or accessibility needs.\n\n## higher education\n\nthe harvard physics study compared a particular tutor at home with active learning in class across two topics and reported higher immediate post-test scores for the tutor condition. the complete-case requirements, one course and changed mode and environment limit generalization. [s08](07-source-records.md#s08)\n\nthe online mba preprint studied one finance course and a course-grounded tutor against access to ordinary resources, including consumer ai. its researcher assessment had substantial missingness; a separate instructor final covered all randomized students. it does not prove that voice or a particular interface generally produces deeper learning. [s21](07-source-records.md#s21)\n\n## professional learning\n\nin peru, a randomized package for sixth-grade teachers combined training, a licence and follow-up. the authors measured teacher ai-related skills and reported no time savings on seven core tasks. they did not establish an improvement in pupils’ learning. professional learning and student learning should not be conflated. [s16](07-source-records.md#s16)\n\na medical-school deployment studied usage and feedback, not a randomized learning outcome. some students used the tool for clarification, but the researchers did not systematically assess output accuracy and could not reconstruct individual use patterns from the conversation-level data. [s10](07-source-records.md#s10)\n\n## missing perspectives\n\nthere is limited primary evidence in this collection about older adult learners, foundational literacy programs, apprenticeships, disabled adult learners and communities with limited connectivity. involving these learners in design is a proposed practice; research on outcomes and unintended effects is still needed. [s14](07-source-records.md#s14) [s15](07-source-records.md#s15)\n", "number": "21"}, {"path": "22-reading-studies.html", "title": "Study design: reading claims carefully", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "evidence", "text": "# study design: reading claims carefully\n\nevidence can be useful without answering every question. a careful reader can separate what a study observed, what its design supports, and what remains a proposal.\n\n## who and what was randomized?\n\na trial may assign people, tutors, classrooms or schools. participants in a cluster share conditions, so the number of learners is not automatically the number of independent randomized units. tutor copilot assigned tutors; the peru study assigned schools after network-level selection. the design and analysis must account for this structure. [s19](07-source-records.md#s19) [s16](07-source-records.md#s16)\n\n## how many outcomes were actually observed?\n\nenrollment, randomization and outcome completion can differ. the computer-science preprint randomized 979 course enrollees but analyzed 431 complete cases across all phases. a reduced sample may differ from those missing; it does not become 979 independent observations because that many were initially assigned. [s20](07-source-records.md#s20)\n\n## what was the alternative?\n\na program may bundle extra time, trained adults and technology. the nigeria study compares its combined after-school package with ordinary instruction; it cannot isolate the effect of the model from those other ingredients. a general tool, a teacher-informed tutor and a human tutor may all be meaningful comparators for different questions. [s09](07-source-records.md#s09)\n\n## what did the test actually measure?\n\nan exit ticket, an exam, a prompt-quality rubric, a self-report and a conversation count are not interchangeable. a short-term assessment can reveal an immediate difference without proving lasting retention or transfer. a finding that is not statistically significant is not proof that two approaches are equal. a source’s policy recommendation is not a measured intervention effect. [s01](07-source-records.md#s01) [s19](07-source-records.md#s19)\n\n## what was accessible?\n\nan abstract may omit recruitment, missing data or adverse outcomes; a dataset card is not necessarily the paper. access limitations in the [source records](07-source-records.md) describe the material dux actually inspected. an inaccessible supplement is an unknown, not evidence of an absence.\n", "number": "22"}, {"path": "23-access-and-use.html", "title": "Implementation: access is not the same as use", "kind": "synthesis", "topic": "children", "text": "# implementation: access is not the same as use\n\nan education system may make a tool available without learners being able or willing to use it as intended. time, access, relationships, technical reliability, privacy, motivation and the fit between activity and learner can all affect implementation. usage is an outcome to examine, not proof that learning occurred.\n\n## two elementary-school trials\n\na 2026 working paper reports two randomized trials in which children used the same ai reading platform independently or with an in-person tutor supporting engagement. across the districts, 355 children were assigned; the programs differed in setting and tutor type. the human-support group spent slightly more time on the platform and read more stories, but overall usage remained low. exploratory reading scores did not significantly improve. the comparison does not tell us whether ai reading instruction beats a human tutor or no intervention. [s22](07-source-records.md#s22)\n\n## ask what happened in practice\n\na pilot can document the number offered access, who actually takes part, how often, what support is needed, and why people decline or stop. separate these observations from whether learning improved. participation data can themselves be sensitive, so collect only what is necessary and make reporting transparent. these are evaluation proposals, not measured outcomes of a dux pilot. [s11](07-source-records.md#s11) [s13](07-source-records.md#s13)\n\n## avoid a simple dose story\n\nthe provider of the studied platform offered a recommended usage level. that statement is a provider claim; the two trials do not establish a universal safe or effective number of minutes. increased screen time may displace another useful activity. compare the full learning experience, including human relationships and accessible alternatives, before deciding whether more use is desirable. [s22](07-source-records.md#s22)\n", "number": "23"}, {"path": "source-s01.html", "title": "Learning performance and unassisted assessment", "kind": "source", "topic": "independence", "text": "# learning performance and unassisted assessment\n\n**source:** hamsa bastani, osbert bastani, alp sungu, haosen ge, özge kabakcı and rei mariman, “generative ai without guardrails can harm learning: evidence from high school mathematics,” *pnas* 122(26), e2422633122, 25 june 2025, [doi:10.1073/pnas.2422633122](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2422633122). the [20 august 2025 correction](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2518204122) was inspected: it corrects osbert bastani’s affiliation after a production error; it reports no changes to findings.\n\n**access and design:** publisher article and the relevant sections of the [author-hosted manuscript and appendix](https://hamsabastani.github.io/education_llm.pdf) inspected. the publisher-hosted supplement returned http 403; equivalence to the author-hosted version was not independently established. the preregistration itself was not inspected. preregistered classroom-assigned field experiment at one turkish high school in fall 2023, grades 9–11. four 90-minute sessions; approximately 50 classes and nearly 1,000 real students, not model-generated participants. the author-hosted appendix table 3 reports 839 students in the main survey sample: 277 gpt tutor, 320 control, and 242 gpt base; its survey sample including honors contains 943 students. the author version says the main sample excludes honors classes and nonrespondents to the baseline survey. these counts should not be described as total enrollment. the publisher main table 1 reports 2,848 student-session observations per outcome, not 2,848 people. exact enrollment and class counts remain unverified. unit of assignment: classroom; student-session performance analyzed, with classroom-clustered standard errors. honors classes excluded from main sample. students worked from teacher-provided problems, with notes/textbooks alone, a gpt-4 chat interface, or a teacher-informed gpt-4 tutor including solutions and hints, then took an unassisted exam.\n\n**finding:** relative to controls, practice scores were higher with both ai variants; the standard interface group scored 17% lower on the subsequent unassisted exam relative to controls. the guarded tutor group did not show a positive exam effect relative to controls, and the negative effect was largely mitigated. these are short-term results for this setting and these specific tools; they do not establish long-term outcomes, effects on all ages, or that every guardrail works. mechanism analyses suggest copying or reduced engagement, but do not definitively isolate the mechanism. see [publisher methods and results](https://www.pnas.org/doi/10.1073/pnas.2422633122).", "number": "01"}, {"path": "source-s02.html", "title": "Student AI competencies", "kind": "source", "topic": "literacy", "text": "# student ai competencies\n\n**source:** fengchun miao, kelly shiohira and natalie lao, unesco, *ai competency framework for students* (2024), [official publication page](https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/ai-competency-framework-students?hub=66682); [full framework, doi 10.54675/jkjb9835](https://doi.org/10.54675/jkjb9835).\n\n**access and design:** official overview and parts of framework pdf inspected. normative curriculum framework, not a trial or a participant sample. twelve competencies span human-centred mindset, ethics, ai techniques and applications, and system design, across understand, apply and create. it recommends agency and critical judgment. this specifies educational goals; it does not show that any particular curriculum causes improved learning.", "number": "02"}, {"path": "source-s03.html", "title": "Educator competencies", "kind": "source", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# educator competencies\n\n**source:** unesco, *ai competency framework for teachers* (2024), [official framework pdf](https://unesdoc.unesco.org/in/rest/annotationsvc/downloadwatermarkedattachment/attach_import_d418160e-3eb2-4d1c-8239-2744911cc721?_=391104eng.pdf&from=1&to=52).\n\n**access and design:** selected pdf sections inspected; normative professional-development framework, not a controlled outcome study. it emphasizes teacher agency, privacy, critical review, human interaction and learner control. its recommendation to test pedagogical strategies is a program-design principle, not proof of efficacy.", "number": "03"}, {"path": "source-s04.html", "title": "Age-appropriate implementation", "kind": "source", "topic": "children", "text": "# age-appropriate implementation\n\n**source:** unesco, *guidance for generative ai in education and research*, 7 september 2023, [official overview](https://www.unesco.org/en/articles/guidance-generative-ai-education-and-research?hub=67098).\n\n**access and design:** official overview inspected; full guidance not fully reviewed. policy and pedagogical guidance, not a trial. it calls for privacy protections, age limits for independent conversations, and age-appropriate validation and design. specific age thresholds and current jurisdiction-specific legal requirements have not been verified here; avoid presenting any recommendation as a universal law.", "number": "04"}, {"path": "source-s05.html", "title": "Children's rights and safeguards", "kind": "source", "topic": "children", "text": "# children's rights and safeguards\n\n**source:** unicef innocenti, *guidance on ai and children*, version 3.0, december 2025, [official report page](https://www.unicef.org/innocenti/reports/policy-guidance-ai-children).\n\n**access and design:** issuer's overview inspected; complete pdf not reviewed. rights-based guidance informed by consultation and existing evidence, including a twelve-country study; that number is countries, not a participant count. the overview lists safety, privacy, fairness, transparency, accountability, development, inclusion and children's ai skills among ten requirements. these are recommendations, not evidence that a particular safeguard has been validated or legal advice.", "number": "05"}, {"path": "source-s06.html", "title": "Conversational AI and children", "kind": "source", "topic": "children", "text": "# conversational ai and children\n\n**source:** unicef, *when ai becomes a friend: child rights risks, harms, and regulatory responses to ai chatbots and companions*, june 2026, [official document page](https://www.unicef.org/documents/when-ai-becomes-friend-child-rights-risks).\n\n**access and design:** issuer overview inspected, not full policy brief. policy analysis compares approaches across six jurisdictions as of may 2026; six is a jurisdiction count, not children or experiments. it identifies risks and recommends preventive safeguards and accountability. the overview does not quantify risk or establish prevalence.", "number": "06"}, {"path": "source-s07.html", "title": "A short literacy intervention", "kind": "source", "topic": "literacy", "text": "# a short literacy intervention\n\n**source:** brett puppart and jaan aru, *a brief ai literacy intervention does not significantly reduce over-reliance and increases under-reliance on chatgpt: a randomized study*, [arxiv:2503.10556v2](https://arxiv.org/abs/2503.10556v2), revised 18 september 2026; originally submitted 13 march 2025 under a different title. doi [10.48550/arxiv.2503.10556](https://doi.org/10.48550/arxiv.2503.10556).\n\n**access and design:** selected methods, results and limitations of the 28-page [v2 pdf](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2503.10556v2) inspected, as well as the version history. preprint; peer-review or subsequent publication status not established. in october 2024 researchers invited 72 estonian schools; 10 forwarded invitations. forty-two real 12th-grade students completed the questionnaire; six were excluded for stated speed criteria, leaving 36 analyzed, ages 18–19: 19 intervention and 17 control. individuals were randomized. each saw eight selected math puzzles and fixed screenshots of model recommendations, four correct and four incorrect, and decided whether to adopt them; they did not interact live with the model. the control read a general introduction and the intervention received additional information. participant and trial are distinct units.\n\n**finding and limitations:** across participants, the reported mean proportion of incorrect recommendations adopted was 52.1%. the intervention group’s mean was 48.68% versus 55.88% for controls; the difference was not statistically significant (p = .471), which does not establish equivalence or no effect. rejection of correct advice was higher in the intervention group (36.84% versus 19.12%; p = .025). the small sample, selection, exclusion criteria, fixed screenshots and short task constrain precision and generalization; the study does not establish that ai literacy education generally fails, or what happens in ordinary iterative chatbot use.", "number": "07"}, {"path": "source-s08.html", "title": "Undergraduate physics tutor trial", "kind": "source", "topic": "adult", "text": "# undergraduate physics tutor trial\n\n**source:** greg kestin, kelly miller, anna klales, timothy milbourne and gregorio ponti, “ai tutoring outperforms in-class active learning: an rct introducing a novel research-based design in an authentic educational setting,” *scientific reports* 15, 17458, 3 june 2025. [doi:10.1038/s41598-025-97652-6](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-025-97652-6).\n\n**access and design:** publisher article methods and results inspected; supplement not inspected. two-week crossover randomized experiment in one harvard undergraduate introductory physics course, fall 2023. of 233 enrolled students, 194 met inclusion criteria involving consent, participation in both modes and completed tests. peer-instruction groups of 2–3 were assigned together. the study compared an ai tutor used at home with in-class active learning on two topics; its tutor used pre-written answers and content-specific prompting. the reported post-test counts are 142 ai-condition and 174 classroom-condition lesson observations, not distinct people.\n\n**finding and limits:** the paper reports higher immediate post-test performance in the tutor condition. the environments, delivery and conditions differ together; this does not isolate ai from all other differences, measure long-term retention, establish effects in other learners or show that ai generally replaces teachers.", "number": "08"}, {"path": "source-s09.html", "title": "Nigeria after-school pilot", "kind": "source", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# nigeria after-school pilot\n\n**source:** martin elias de simone, federico hernan tiberti, maria barron rodriguez, federico manolio, wuraola mosuro and eliot jolomi dikoru, *from chalkboards to chatbots: evaluating the impact of generative ai on learning outcomes in nigeria*, world bank education global department working paper, may 2025. [working paper pdf](https://thedocs.worldbank.org/en/doc/0c08ef70ff0e1d81997f116923c0e2c7-0140022025/related/edu-wp-18-evaluating-the-impact-of-generative-ai-on-learning-outcomes-in-nigeria.pdf); [reproducibility package doi](https://doi.org/10.60572/cre7-j348).\n\n**access and design:** working paper methods and results and reproducibility-package overview inspected; data and code not independently rerun. interested first-year senior secondary students in benin city, nigeria, were individually randomized: 657 to a six-week teacher-supported after-school ai program and 671 to usual schooling. the endline assessment included 422 treatment and 337 control students, 759 total; these are people, not sessions. the program combined materials, adult support and gpt-4-based copilot. the control did not receive an equal-time alternative.\n\n**finding and limits:** the authors report a 0.31 standard-deviation improvement in a combined english, ai-knowledge and digital-skills assessment and 0.238 standard deviations in english; the relevant regression reports 654 observations, fewer than the 759 endline completers. differential attrition was significant; the paper reports sensitivity analyses, but selection remains a limitation. the bundled intervention does not establish the separate effect of ai, nor its long-term impact. the working paper’s projected earnings are projections, not observed participant outcomes.", "number": "09"}, {"path": "source-s10.html", "title": "Medical education observational study", "kind": "source", "topic": "adult", "text": "# medical education observational study\n\n**source:** thomas thesen and soo hwan park, “a generative ai teaching assistant for personalized learning in medical education,” *npj digital medicine* 8, 627, 4 november 2025. [doi:10.1038/s41746-025-02022-1](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41746-025-02022-1).\n\n**access and design:** publisher methods, selected results and limitations inspected. observational deployment of a course-material-grounded assistant across two medical-school cohorts, not a randomized learning-outcome trial. among survey respondents, 22 of 56 in the first cohort and 23 of 87 in the second reported at least one use. the paper reports 328 conversations in thematic analysis (216 / 65.9% in its first topic category); conversations are not distinct participants, and topic codes can overlap. gpt-4o assisted thematic coding; a 15% subset received human validation. the authors did not systematically assess answer accuracy or track individual engagement patterns. findings describe usage and reported experience in this setting; they do not establish learning gains or that retrieval eliminates hallucination.", "number": "10"}, {"path": "source-s11.html", "title": "Evaluating education-technology privacy", "kind": "source", "topic": "safety", "text": "# evaluating education-technology privacy\n\n**source:** u.s. department of education, student privacy policy office, [privacy and education technology](https://studentprivacy.ed.gov/privacy-and-education-technology), webpage, publication date not established; accessed 12 november 2026.\n\n**access and design:** agency overview inspected, linked model terms and underlying legal guidance not individually reviewed. the page links resources on assessing how online services collect, use and transmit information. this is a us agency resource, not a study or a complete statement of requirements in every jurisdiction. laws and interpretations can change.", "number": "11"}, {"path": "source-s12.html", "title": "Child privacy and schools", "kind": "source", "topic": "safety", "text": "# child privacy and schools\n\n**source:** u.s. federal trade commission, [complying with coppa: frequently asked questions](https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/resources/complying-coppa-frequently-asked-questions), section n, webpage accessed 12 november 2026.\n\n**access and design:** selected agency faq on schools inspected; not a trial. it describes limited circumstances in which a school may act for a parent in relation to educational services, and distinguishes school-benefit uses from other commercial purposes. the detailed requirements and applicable law depend on context and may change. dux does not provide legal advice or assert that this us guidance applies elsewhere.", "number": "12"}, {"path": "source-s13.html", "title": "AI risk management framework", "kind": "source", "topic": "safety", "text": "# ai risk management framework\n\n**source:** elham tabassi, national institute of standards and technology, *artificial intelligence risk management framework (ai rmf 1.0)*, 26 january 2023, [doi:10.6028/nist.ai.100-1](https://doi.org/10.6028/nist.ai.100-1); [core functions](https://airc.nist.gov/airmf-resources/airmf/5-sec-core/).\n\n**access and design:** nist publication overview and online core inspected. voluntary, non-sector-specific governance framework, not a school efficacy study or a binding universal standard. it organizes activities around govern, map, measure and manage, and says the functions are not a checklist. nist notes a revision is in progress; check current status before use.", "number": "13"}, {"path": "source-s14.html", "title": "Web accessibility guidance", "kind": "source", "topic": "access", "text": "# web accessibility guidance\n\n**source:** world wide web consortium, *web content accessibility guidelines 2.2*, [w3c recommendation, 12 december 2024](https://www.w3.org/tr/2024/rec-wcag22-20241212/).\n\n**access and design:** recommendation overview and publication history inspected. technical accessibility standard, not an empirical evaluation of an ai tutor. w3c states its guidelines do not address every need. conformance of any dux or third-party service has not been established by inclusion here.", "number": "14"}, {"path": "source-s15.html", "title": "Education technology in context", "kind": "source", "topic": "access", "text": "# education technology in context\n\n**source:** unesco global education monitoring report team, *technology in education: a tool on whose terms?*, 2023, [official publication page](https://www.unesco.org/gem-report/en/publication/technology?hub=83250).\n\n**access and design:** issuer’s overview and selected summary text inspected, not every underlying study. the report considers education technology through relevance, equity, scalability and sustainability. its broader education-technology scope must not be recast as a trial of generative ai. it advocates learning centred on people and attention to potential adverse consequences.", "number": "15"}, {"path": "source-s16.html", "title": "Teacher AI program in Peru", "kind": "source", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# teacher ai program in peru\n\n**source:** carla z. glave, carolina lopez, ezequiel molina and román andrés zárate, *not faster, but better: generative ai and teacher skill in peru 2025*, world bank dataset description, 20 july 2026, [doi:10.48529/t6x6-2y70](https://doi.org/10.48529/t6x6-2y70).\n\n**access and design:** primary data catalogue description and abstract inspected; full paper, dataset and preregistration not inspected. the record describes 1,269 sixth-grade teachers across 390 public schools in metropolitan lima, with school-network eligibility and school-level randomization. it identifies a program combining training, a copilot licence and follow-up, with further randomized message features. the catalogue abstract reports greater adoption without measured time savings on core tasks; this is a bundled program claim, not evidence on student learning or on ai software in isolation. the catalogue is a research lead: outcome measures, attrition, uncertainty and paper version require full-paper verification before stronger claims.\n\n**update, 12 november 2026:** the [october 2026 working paper](https://documents.worldbank.org/curated/en/099309210012625906/pdf/idu-c261aadb-6c32-48af-a07c-9cff5e86c716.pdf), policy research working paper 11476, doi [10.1596/1813-9450-11476](https://doi.org/10.1596/1813-9450-11476), is now accessible; selected methods, results and tables were inspected. of 390 schools, 196 (660 teachers) were assigned the package and 194 (609 teachers) control. controls retained access to publicly available ai. the authors report higher scores on prompt rubrics and better judgments of weak ai output, and no significant time reduction across seven self-reported tasks. time analysis table 3 lists 607 observations; other analyses have different denominators. the authors report no differential endline response, which does not remove all selection concerns. student learning was not established. dataset and code were not rerun; the research was funded in part by the microsoft ai economy institute. the earlier catalogue-only access note is retained as the record of the initial review.", "number": "16"}, {"path": "source-s17.html", "title": "Undergraduate preparation and belonging", "kind": "source", "topic": "adult", "text": "# undergraduate preparation and belonging\n\n**source:** renlin wang, jian sun and shu wen, “structured generative ai preparation and classroom belonging in a six-class randomized trial,” *scientific reports*, accepted article published online 26 october 2026, [doi:10.1038/s41598-026-78039-7](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41598-026-78039-7).\n\n**access and design:** publisher abstract inspected; complete methods, data and supplement not independently reviewed. the publisher labels it an accepted version subject to further editing. six intact classes at one chinese university were randomized, three sections and 110 students per condition. the intervention combines ai exploration, confidence reflection, question generation and instructor responsiveness. it measures classroom belonging and related reported outcomes; the section is the assignment unit, and the number of sections is six, not 220 independent randomized units.\n\n**finding and limits:** the abstract reports a difference in belonging at week 12 under its section-level analysis, while its exact two-sided randomization tests give p = .100 for the primary contrasts. the complex intervention does not isolate ai or establish effects in other settings. this is a limited-access research lead pending full methods review, not a broad claim that ai improves belonging.", "number": "17"}, {"path": "source-s18.html", "title": "OECD and European Commission AI literacy framework", "kind": "source", "topic": "literacy", "text": "# oecd and european commission ai literacy framework\n\n**source:** oecd and european commission, *empowering learners for the age of ai: an ai literacy framework for primary and secondary education*, 18 june 2026, [doi:10.1787/65cd27d4-en](https://doi.org/10.1787/65cd27d4-en).\n\n**access and design:** official overview and abstract inspected; complete 64-page framework not fully reviewed. normative framework for desired learning outcomes; no participant count or intervention effect. it includes understanding, critical evaluation and ethical and creative use. it complements, rather than experimentally validates, unesco’s framework. specific activities and age progression require review of the full report.", "number": "18"}, {"path": "source-s19.html", "title": "AI support for human tutors", "kind": "source", "topic": "teaching", "text": "# ai support for human tutors\n\n**source:** rose e. wang, ana t. ribeiro, carly d. robinson, susanna loeb and dora demszky, *tutor copilot: a human-ai approach for scaling real-time expertise*, [arxiv:2410.03017](https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.03017), 3 october 2024 version inspected. peer-review and subsequent version status not established.\n\n**access and design:** preprint methods and selected results inspected; preregistration and data not independently checked. two-month study in a southern us district, nine schools and grades 3–8. the district identified 1,787 students; 900 tutors were initially randomized, 450 per arm; at study launch 782 remained, 386 treatment and 396 control. the text also reports 874 full-time tutors assigned to schools, a separate figure whose relationship to the launch sample is not fully clarified here. randomization was by tutor, not student. final analysis comprises 4,136 sessions and over 550,000 chat messages, neither being a count of individuals. intervention tutors could request ai-generated suggestions; control tutors taught without it. the lead author worked part-time with the provider.\n\n**finding and limits:** the paper reports exit-ticket pass rate of 62% control versus 66% treatment, a four percentage-point intention-to-treat difference. it does not establish lasting achievement, effects in other settings, or safety of unsupervised child-facing chatbots. tutors reported some suggestions were not grade appropriate. classifier-based analysis of chat language and tutor interviews are different kinds of evidence from the exit-ticket outcome.\n\n**update, 12 november 2026:** a later [november 2025 working-paper version](https://edworkingpapers.com/sites/default/files/ai24_1054_v2.pdf) was located and selected methods and limitations inspected. it reports 900 tutors initially assigned; 783 with study sessions (388 treatment, 395 control), 1,534 students initially eligible and 1,013 attending at least one study session, in grades 3–6. these differ from the 2024 version’s figures above; do not merge versions or silently substitute counts. in the newer version students could be matched with different tutors across sessions. it reports the same direction for the session-level exit-ticket outcome; the authors report no statistically significant end-of-year math effect and caution that their tutor-randomized design cannot directly estimate a student-level treatment effect on that outcome. the later version’s different sample definitions and coding figures require version-specific citation.", "number": "19"}, {"path": "source-s20.html", "title": "Prompting instruction in introductory computer science", "kind": "source", "topic": "literacy", "text": "# prompting instruction in introductory computer science\n\n**source:** ruiwei xiao, runlong ye, xinying hou, jessica wen, harsh kumar, michael liut and john stamper, *transforming genai policy to prompting instruction: an rct of scalable prompting interventions in a cs1 course*, [arxiv:2602.16033](https://arxiv.org/abs/2602.16033), 17 february 2026 version inspected. preprint; peer-review or later version not established.\n\n**access and design:** preprint methods and selected results inspected. at one north american university, 979 enrolled students were randomized to four instruction conditions; 431 with valid responses across all phases formed the complete-case sample (102, 112, 109 and 108 respectively). this 44% complete-case rate limits interpretation. students generated prompts assessed against the researchers’ rubric; the most active condition included gpt-4o feedback. the authors report gains in the measured prompting task and no direct final-exam differences between conditions. the relationship between prompting gains and exam scores is observational within the study and does not establish that prompting practice caused higher course performance.", "number": "20"}, {"path": "source-s21.html", "title": "Structured tutoring and modality in an online MBA", "kind": "source", "topic": "adult", "text": "# structured tutoring and modality in an online mba\n\n**source:** shihao yang, marshall van alstyne and chrysanthos dellarocas, *when ai tutors speak: evidence from a randomized field experiment*, [arxiv:2609.23958](https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.23958), working draft dated 20 september 2026. preprint; peer review not established.\n\n**access and design:** methods and selected results of the draft inspected. in one online mba finance course, 86 consenting students were randomized to a structured course-grounded tutor or a holdout that could use consumer ai. fifty-one completed both study assessment waves; the paper also reports instructor gradebook outcomes for all 86. within the tutor arm, modality alternated weekly. the paper reports 32 students in the within-student voice/text comparison. students, assessment waves, weekly interactions and course examinations are distinct units.\n\n**finding and limits:** the researchers report an advantage for tutor access on their assessment and a smaller same-direction difference on the instructor’s final. attrition affects the custom-assessment analysis; the final was not itself a preregistered outcome. no general conclusion about all adults or all voice tutors follows, and a non-significant modality difference does not by itself prove equivalence. the tutor, subject, cohort and short field period bound the findings.", "number": "21"}, {"path": "source-s22.html", "title": "Human engagement support for elementary reading", "kind": "source", "topic": "children", "text": "# human engagement support for elementary reading\n\n**source:** carly d. robinson, david gormley, ana trindade ribeiro and susanna loeb, *access is not enough: human support improves engagement with ai tutoring*, edworkingpaper 26-1451, june 2026, [doi:10.26300/pz7p-p388](https://doi.org/10.26300/pz7p-p388); [author-hosted paper](https://scale.stanford.edu/sites/default/files/ai26-1451.pdf).\n\n**access and design:** working-paper methods and selected results inspected; preregistration and underlying data not independently checked. two individually randomized trials compared an ai reading platform with the same platform plus in-person engagement support. district a assigned 84 treatment and 90 control students in grades 1–5; district b assigned 36 treatment and 145 control students in grades 1–3, 355 children total. the human role focused on engagement and troubleshooting, not direct reading instruction; scheduled intervention structures differed. the study is not a comparison of ai tutoring with human instruction or no tutoring.\n\n**finding and limits:** the paper reports roughly one additional minute per week of platform use in district a and 4.4 in district b, with low usage overall. exploratory reading achievement outcomes were not significantly different; this does not prove no effect. districts, measures and implementation varied, and the provider’s recommended usage level is a provider claim, not an established universal threshold.", "number": "22"}];
